{
  "case_id": "2017-16-534",
  "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:11 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ZACHARY D. TRIPP, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States as amicus curiae, supporting the Respondents"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0000-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In 2008, Congress comprehensively overhauled the terrorism exception to foreign sovereign immunity to close gaps that had for years allowed foreign terror states to thumb their noses at U.S. judgments finding them liable for acts of terrorism while their victims were drawn into a long, bitter, and often futile search for scarce assets that would be subject to execution under the exceedingly narrow commercial exception to foreign sovereign immunity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Asher Perlin (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The centerpiece of that legislation is Section 1610(g). That provision provides that American terrorism victims can execute their judgments upon the property of a foreign state"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Asher Perlin (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress, when they enacted 1610(g), they did not completely abrogate foreign sovereign immunity for terror states. They wanted to provide a remedy for the victims, they wanted to punish and deter the terror states, but at the same time, Congress recognized that Iran and North Korea, Syria, Sudan, these are sovereign states, and they're entitled to a bare minimum of sovereign immunity, and Congress retained that bare minimum by protecting quintessentially sovereign assets while making everything else subject to execution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm sorry. I thought that the University of Chicago had raised an interesting argument, that the definition of foreign state in the statute includes, by definition, an agency or instrumentality of a foreign state, so that the reference to foreign state that you're relying upon does include the concept of piercing the corporate veil in its very definition."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is -- is there anything in the legislative record that shows that Congress was intending to do anything other than dispense with the Bancec?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "beneficial ownership. That any property in which the foreign state has a beneficial ownership is subject to execution of that judgment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STRAUSS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Let me first pick up on a piece of the legislative history that my friend quoted to the Court. Senator Specter, who introduced the precursor of what became 1610(g), did say that, as -- as Mr. Perlin said, that the provision was designed to eliminate many of the barriers which are preventing U.S. citizens from collecting on court-ordered damages."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. TRIPP: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: These ancient Persian artifacts are immune from execution under 1609, and nothing in 1610(g) lifts that immunity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I know you're saying it, but explain to me why."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government and University",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The first point I want to make is that the -- the government and the University claim that our reading would render this -- would render subsections (a)(7) and (b)(3) superfluous. That's -- that's not the case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Iran",
      "case_id": "2017-16-534",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3a29d12a7507d03c24a2831d78b13c75334da576cb6147f66173b45a0b8bf823",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3a29d12a7507-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Iran defended, and they said you can't -- you cannot enforce your judgment under 1610(b)(3) because that applies only where the judgment is entered against the instrument -\u00ad the agency or instrumentality, and Bank Melli -- there's no judgment here. That's what Iran -- that's what Iran's argument was. The -- and, right -- Iran continues to maintain that -- that it won't apply to (b)(3)."
    },
